    <body>
      <sec id="article-140257.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome, or Lowe syndrome, is a rare X-linked recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene, leading to disruption of phosphoinositide metabolism and widespread cellular dysfunction. This course reviews this condition, which affects multiple organ systems, producing a characteristic triad of congenital cataracts, intellectual disability, and proximal renal tubular dysfunction, as well as the neurological manifestations of oculocerebrorenal syndrome, including hypotonia, seizures, and behavioral disturbances. Additionally, renal involvement progresses to chronic kidney disease.&#x000a0;Ocular findings such as bilateral cataracts and infantile-onset glaucoma that frequently cause severe visual impairment and the need for supportive,&#x000a0;interprofessional management for optimizing function and quality of life are also discussed.</p>
        <p>This course explores the molecular pathogenesis, clinical manifestations, diagnostic strategies, and management principles of oculocerebrorenal syndrome. Participants also gain an in-depth understanding of the roles of the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene and its enzyme product, OCRL-1, in regulating intracellular trafficking and cytoskeletal organization.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in early recognition of systemic involvement, evidence-based approaches for managing ocular and renal complications, performing the recommended evaluation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach to optimize long-term outcomes for patients affected by this complex multisystem disorder.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate&#x000a0;Oculocerebrorenal syndrome from other causes of failure to thrive and congenital cataracts based on clinical, ocular, and systemic features.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identify key genetic and phenotypic markers that aid in timely diagnosis and individualized management planning.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess the role of early interprofessional intervention in improving long-term visual, renal, and neurodevelopmental outcomes in affected children.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Enhance the interprofessional approach in the diagnosis, management, and long-term care of patients with oculocerebrorenal syndrome by fostering collaboration among pediatricians, ophthalmologists, nephrologists, geneticists, and allied health professionals.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=140257&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=140257">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome, more commonly referred to as Lowe syndrome, was first described in the early 1950s by Lowe and colleagues, who identified a constellation of findings in affected children, including intellectual disability, organic aciduria, reduced renal ammonia production, bilateral cataracts, and glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r1">[1]</xref> Subsequent studies have further delineated the disorder, noting the presence of Fanconi-type proximal renal tubulopathy, generalized areflexia, and characteristic arthropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref> The condition is now established as an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene, leading to disturbances in phosphoinositide metabolism and widespread impairment of intracellular trafficking and cytoskeletal organization. These molecular derangements explain the syndrome&#x02019;s broad involvement of ocular, neurological, and renal systems.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>The syndrome primarily affects the lens and trabecular meshwork in the eye, resulting in congenital cataracts and infantile-onset glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r5">[5]</xref> In the central nervous system, abnormalities in synaptic signaling and white matter integrity underlie the intellectual disability, hypotonia, and seizures observed in many patients. Renal manifestations arise from dysfunction of the proximal tubules, producing a partial Fanconi syndrome characterized by bicarbonaturia, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria, and progressive chronic kidney disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The natural history of oculocerebrorenal syndrome typically begins in the neonatal period, characterized by severe muscular hypotonia and ocular abnormalities, which serve as the earliest diagnostic indicators.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref> Renal manifestations generally evolve more gradually, with incomplete Fanconi syndrome becoming evident in infancy or early childhood. Over time, the progression of renal disease culminates in chronic kidney failure, which remains the leading cause of mortality. Life expectancy is limited, with most patients surviving into the third or fourth decade of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref> Early diagnosis and supportive care are crucial for preventing life-threatening complications and maximizing the quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The oculocerebrorenal syndrome is caused by mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene, which is mapped to chromosome Xq25&#x02013;26.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;The <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene encodes the enzyme OCRL-1, an inositol polyphosphate 5-phosphatase, that primarily converts phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2) to phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref> OCRL-1 is localized to clathrin-coated pits on the plasma membrane, as well as to multiple intracellular organelles, including endosomes and the trans-Golgi network.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r10">[10]</xref> Through these localizations, OCRL-1 regulates endocytic trafficking, Golgi-to-endosome transport, actin cytoskeleton remodeling, and polarized cell maturation, processes that are essential for cellular protein sorting, signaling, and structural integrity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>The mutation spectrum of <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> is broad, with more than 200 pathogenic variants reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r12">[12]</xref> The majority are frameshift, nonsense, or splice-site mutations that lead to mRNA instability or premature protein truncation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Missense mutations and larger genomic deletions account for a smaller proportion of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;The <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic>&#x000a0;mutation exhibits considerable phenotypic heterogeneity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>The inheritance pattern of oculocerebrorenal syndrome is X-linked recessive.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r17">[17]</xref> Affected males typically inherit the pathogenic variant from carrier mothers, although de novo mutations account for up to one-third of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r18">[18]</xref> Female carriers are usually asymptomatic, but approximately 95% of those older than 15 years exhibit subtle but characteristic radial lenticular opacities on slit-lamp examination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;In rare instances, female carriers may manifest systemic features of oculocerebrorenal syndrome, particularly in the presence of cytogenetic or epigenetic alterations. Such scenarios include a 45, X karyotype, skewed X-inactivation favoring the mutant allele, compound heterozygosity for&#x000a0;2 pathogenic variants, or X-autosome translocations disrupting <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Recent evidence suggests that INPP5B, a closely related inositol 5-phosphatase paralog of OCRL-1, may act as a functional modifier in oculocerebrorenal syndrome and Dent disease type 2.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;In addition to oculocerebrorenal syndrome, mutations in <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> can result in&#x000a0;a rare&#x000a0;X-linked recessive condition known as Dent disease type 2. This allelic disorder has a milder phenotype and is caused by&#x000a0;missense variants distributed across exons 9 to 21, while truncating mutations tend to cluster in exons 1 to 7.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r19">[19]</xref> Patients with Dent disease&#x000a0;type 2 typically present with isolated renal Fanconi syndrome, low molecular weight proteinuria, and hypercalciuria without the classic extrarenal features, including ocular or neurological manifestations of oculocerebrorenal syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Ocular features are uncommon in Dent disease type 2 and include mild nuclear cataracts, which are usually not visually significant. Typically,&#x000a0;proximal renal tubular acidosis is not seen in Dent disease type 2.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome is a rare disorder, with an estimated prevalence of approximately 1 in 500,000 in the general population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;This condition occurs almost exclusively in males and, within the United States, has been reported to affect between 1 and 10 males per million.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome results from mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene, which encodes the inositol polyphosphate 5-phosphatase OCRL-1. A deficiency of this enzyme disrupts phosphoinositide metabolism, leading to the abnormal accumulation of PIP2 and impairing key cellular processes, including endocytosis, vesicular trafficking, actin cytoskeleton remodeling, cell polarity, and ciliogenesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r10">[10]</xref> These defects are particularly detrimental in highly polarized cells,&#x000a0;eg, renal proximal tubular epithelium, neurons, and lens epithelial cells, which explains the characteristic multisystem involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;The most common clinical manifestations include congenital cataracts, infantile glaucoma, hypotonia with delayed motor development, intellectual disability, and a progressive Fanconi-type proximal renal tubulopathy leading to chronic kidney disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Patients with oculocerebrorenal syndrome exhibit defects across several organ systems. The triad of congenital cataracts, cognitive impairment, and proximal renal tubular dysfunction classically defines it.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r23">[23]</xref> The syndrome's varying clinical manifestations are associated with the following organ systems.</p>
        <p>
<bold>The Nervous System</bold>
</p>
        <p>Neurological involvement is a&#x000a0;leading cause of morbidity in oculocerebrorenal syndrome, with both the central and peripheral nervous systems affected. Early features commonly include profound hypotonia and reduced or absent deep tendon reflexes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Feeding difficulties are common in infancy, often due to weak or ineffective sucking. Hypotonia contributes to the delayed attainment of motor milestones,&#x000a0;eg, independent ambulation. Seizures may develop and can vary widely in type and severity, while intellectual disability is present in most patients, ranging from mild to severe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, behavioral disturbances are also common, with many patients exhibiting aggression, obsessive-compulsive traits, temper outbursts, and other conduct disorders that impair social and daily functioning and may result in self-injury (including head banging).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>The Ocular System</bold>
</p>
        <p>The visual prognosis in patients with oculocerebrorenal syndrome is generally poor, largely due to the coexistence of multiple ocular abnormalities. The hallmark finding is bilateral congenital cataracts, which develop in utero and are typically addressed with cataract surgery during infancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Cataract formation is believed to result from a genetic defect interfering with the migration of crystalline lens epithelial cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Although not specific to oculocerebrorenal syndrome, these children may have associated posterior lenticonus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In addition, some patients develop X-linked infantile glaucoma, with or without buphthalmos, usually within the first few decades of life. This glaucoma is challenging to manage and has been identified as one of the major causes of blindness in oculocerebrorenal syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Infantile glaucoma affects around 50% of patients and can be&#x000a0;challenging to&#x000a0;treat.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref> Patients are often on multiple topical medications.</p>
        <p>Surgical intervention may be necessary for more effective control of intraocular pressure.&#x000a0;In a case series, Shah and colleagues reported distinct anatomic differences in glaucomatous patients with oculocerebrorenal syndrome. These differences include a deeper and wider anterior chamber, a thinner and steeper cornea, a wider angle, a more reflective and tapered iris, and a thinner, narrower, and flatter lens compared to those without glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Other notable complications of oculocerebrorenal syndrome include the development of conjunctival or corneal keloids, which may lead to visually significant corneal scarring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r32">[32]</xref> Because of this risk, contact lenses are generally avoided in these patients. Additional ophthalmic features that have been described include microphthalmos, enophthalmos, band-shaped keratopathy, retinal dystrophy, nystagmus, strabismus, and amblyopia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Radial lenticular opacities involving all cortical layers are characteristic of female <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene carriers and serve as an important ophthalmologic marker for carrier detection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Recent insights from a cohort of southern Chinese patients broaden the known phenotypic and genetic spectrum of oculocerebrorenal syndrome. In a study of 8 affected children, 5 novel variants of <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> were identified.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Of these,&#x000a0;3 nonsense mutations were confirmed as pathogenic, and&#x000a0;2 missense variants of uncertain significance were associated with classic severe phenotypes. Notably, a patient did not present with congenital cataracts, challenging the assumption that ocular features are always present at birth and illustrating an atypical presentation in oculocerebrorenal syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>The Renal System</bold>
</p>
        <p>In oculocerebrorenal syndrome, the renal involvement is defined by Fanconi syndrome with proximal tubular dysfunction and progression to renal failure. Although renal disease is not always evident at birth, it typically becomes apparent in early infancy. Impaired endocytosis within the proximal tubules leads to low-molecular-weight proteinuria, including beta-2-microglobulin, retinol-binding protein, and the lysosomal enzyme N-acetyl glucosaminidase, often reaching the nephrotic range.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref> Low-molecular-weight proteinuria serves as an early indicator of renal involvement and has been detected as soon as the neonatal period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients are also predisposed to hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Increased urinary excretion of amino acids and calcium is commonly observed, with hypercalciuria leading to nephrolithiasis in approximately half of affected individuals. Renal calculi usually consist of calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r35">[35]</xref> Ongoing phosphate wasting contributes to the development of rickets and osteomalacia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Chronic, progressive renal failure remains a central feature of oculocerebrorenal syndrome and necessitates regular monitoring of renal function. By the fourth decade of life, the majority of patients reach stage 4 to 5 chronic kidney disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>The Musculoskeletal System</bold>
</p>
        <p>Musculoskeletal manifestations of oculocerebrorenal syndrome include osteopenia, tenosynovitis, and debilitating arthropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r38">[38]</xref> Osteopenia can be exacerbated by hypophosphatemia and metabolic acidosis, further resulting in pathologic fractures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;These patients are also prone to stunted growth and short stature, which has been postulated to be the result of growth hormone deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Scoliosis has also been reported in these patients and may worsen with age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Other Clinical Features</bold>
</p>
        <p>Dermatologic findings reported in patients with oculocerebrorenal syndrome include benign cystic&#x000a0;lesions, thought to originate from hair follicles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r41">[41]</xref>&#x000a0;The etiology of these skin lesions is poorly understood but has been attributed to elevated levels of lysosomal enzymes in extracellular tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;Dental anomalies, including delayed tooth eruption, enamel hypoplasia, and tooth impaction, have also been reported in oculocerebrorenal syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;Multiple odontogenic keratocysts have also been reported in a patient with oculocerebrorenal syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>About one-third of males have cryptorchidism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;However, puberty and testosterone levels are reportedly normal in most patients. Delayed platelet activation can also lead to dysfunction of primary&#x000a0;hemostasis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Infrequently, patients may have facial dysmorphism. Facial features include&#x000a0;frontal bossing, deep-set eyes, and chubby cheeks. Individuals may also have a fair complexion and a long face.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>
<bold>Laboratory Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Infants&#x000a0;with clinical features suggestive of oculocerebrorenal syndrome (particularly male infants presenting with congenital cataracts, hypotonia, or failure to thrive) must undergo a thorough laboratory evaluation. Serum and urine studies may demonstrate metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia, elevated creatinine, proteinuria, hypercalciuria, and aminoaciduria, all of which are consistent with proximal tubular dysfunction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Additional diagnostics may be indicated if other systemic symptoms or findings are present.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Imaging Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>In cases where neurological involvement is suspected, neuroimaging may be helpful. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) often reveals periventricular and deep white matter hyperintensities on T2-weighted sequences, and mild ventriculomegaly may also be observed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Molecular Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>The definitive diagnosis is established through molecular genetic testing, which involves identifying a pathogenic variant in the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene. Reduced inositol polyphosphate-5-phosphatase activity can also be demonstrated in cultured skin fibroblasts. Antenatal diagnosis is possible when oculocerebrorenal syndrome is suspected: ultrasound may detect fetal cataracts, and elevated maternal serum or amniotic fluid alpha-fetoprotein levels can provide additional supportive evidence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r48">[48]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The oculocerebrorenal syndrome has systemic manifestations that can impact patient morbidity and mortality. Treatment focuses on optimizing system functions and preventing acute illness.</p>
        <p>
<bold>The Nervous and Musculoskeletal System&#x000a0;Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Management of hypotonia in oculocerebrorenal syndrome relies on early initiation of physical therapy and structured rehabilitation programs, which are essential to improve motor function and prevent long-term disability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref> Maintaining joint mobility through regular physiotherapy may help reduce the risk of developing contractures and lead to better musculoskeletal outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref> Prevention and treatment of osteomalacia are achieved with oral supplementation of vitamin D (calcitriol) and phosphate, addressing the underlying metabolic disturbances. In cases where hypotonia leads to significant dysphagia and impaired oral intake, enteral feeding via nasogastric or gastrostomy tube may be required to ensure adequate nutrition.</p>
        <p>Seizure management is tailored to the individual, with anticonvulsant therapy initiated as clinically indicated. Behavioral and psychiatric manifestations are frequent and best addressed through an interprofessional approach, incorporating medical, psychological, and educational interventions. Comprehensive care may involve special education programs, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy to optimize developmental progress and daily functioning. Pharmacologic agents, eg, antipsychotics and antidepressants, should be individualized alongside supportive care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Iotowa and associates demonstrated the role of recombinant human growth hormone in promoting significant growth and weight gain in a child with genetically confirmed oculocerebrorenal syndrome, highlighting its potential as an adjunct therapy alongside standard interprofessional management to improve physical outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r49">[49]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>The Ocular System&#x000a0;Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Congenital cataracts require early surgical intervention, ideally in infancy, to prevent amblyopia and optimize visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;Due to the small size of their globes, these infants are left aphakic and prescribed relevant aphakic refractive correction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Intraocular lens implantation, especially in these small, vulnerable eyes, can increase the risk of developing glaucoma. Moreover, glaucoma in oculocerebrorenal syndrome is often recalcitrant to treatment and requires further surgical intervention. Contact lenses are not the preferred method of aphakia correction in these patients, as they may increase the risk of corneal keloid formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>These children are to be serially followed up to monitor for changes in refractive error and growth of axial length as the child grows, in addition to checking for postoperative complications, eg, visual axis opacification and aphakic glaucoma. In patients with oculocerebrorenal syndrome who develop infantile or aphakic glaucoma, intraocular pressures are often challenging to control with medical therapy alone. In many cases, surgical management is necessary.</p>
        <p>The most commonly reported glaucoma surgeries performed in this patient population are trabeculectomy and goniotomy. The&#x000a0;other common glaucoma surgery in these patients is aqueous tube shunt implantation.&#x000a0;Patching or extraocular muscle&#x000a0;surgery is used to treat&#x000a0;patients with strabismus. Corneal keloids can be surgically removed, particularly if they compromise visual acuity. However, some patients suffer from corneal scarring or require corneal transplantation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Decreased vision secondary to corneal scarring also increases the risk of amblyopia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>The Renal System</bold>
</p>
        <p>Alkali therapy, most commonly with sodium bicarbonate, is essential in the management of acidemia. Regular monitoring of serum calcium and parathyroid hormone levels is necessary to prevent excessive vitamin D supplementation, which can exacerbate hypercalciuria and increase the risk of nephrolithiasis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;For patients who progress to end-stage renal disease, dialysis becomes necessary, although reports of kidney transplantation in oculocerebrorenal syndrome remain limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r52">[52]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <table-wrap id="article-140257.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table. Differential Diagnoses of Oculocerebrorenal Syndrome</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Differential Diagnosis</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Common Features</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Differentiating Features</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dent Disease (Types 1 and 2)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Proximal tubular dysfunction</p></list-item><list-item><p>Low molecular weight proteinuria</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypercalciuria, progressive renal impairment</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A mutation in the <italic toggle="yes">CLCN5</italic> gene causes Dent disease type 1, while a mutation in the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL1</italic> gene causes Dent disease type 2.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Both are rare, X-linked genetic disorders that affect the kidneys, causing symptoms, eg, proteinuria (protein in the urine), hypercalciuria (excess calcium in the urine), kidney stones, and kidney failure.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dent disease type 2 (an allelic disease of oculocerebrorenal syndrome with a milder phenotype) presents with additional nonrenal symptoms, including mild intellectual disability, diminished muscle tone (hypotonia), and eye abnormalities, which are not typically associated with Dent disease type 1.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dent disease typically lacks ocular (cataracts, glaucoma) and neurological involvement; Dent type 2 may exhibit mild lens opacities but usually does not result in significant visual impairment.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Fanconi Syndrome </bold>(other causes such as&#x000a0;Wilson disease, tyrosinemia, galactosemia, and glycogen storage diseases)</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Renal proximal tubulopathy with aminoaciduria</p></list-item><list-item><p>Phosphaturia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Glycosuria</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bicarbonaturia</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Lacks the triad of oculocerebrorenal syndrome.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cataract morphology is specific in Wilson disease (sunflower cataract) and galactosemia (oil droplet cataract).</p></list-item><list-item><p>Glycogen storage diseases may be associated with corneal clouding.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Mitochondrial Cytopathies</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypotonia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Developmental delay</p></list-item><list-item><p>Possible renal tubular dysfunction</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Multisystemic with lactic acidosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Myopathy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cardiomyopathy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Variable ocular findings, but not the classic triad of cataracts, CNS, and renal disease</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Smith&#x02013;Lemli&#x02013;Opitz Syndrome</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Developmental delay</p></list-item><list-item><p>Intellectual disability</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypotonia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ocular abnormalities</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Caused by a cholesterol biosynthesis defect</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dysmorphic facies, syndactyly, and genital anomalies</p></list-item><list-item><p>No Fanconi-type renal tubulopathy</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Cystinosis</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Proximal renal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Growth retardation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Photophobia</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Characterized by corneal cystine crystals, not congenital cataracts</p></list-item><list-item><p>Progressive renal failure with cystine accumulation</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Galactosemia</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cataracts in infancy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Failure to thrive</p></list-item><list-item><p>Renal tubular dysfunction</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Metabolic disorder presenting with hepatomegaly</p></list-item><list-item><p>Jaundice</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypoglycemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cataracts resolve with dietary management</p></list-item><list-item><p>No neurological triad typical of oculocerebrorenal syndrome</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Zellweger Spectrum Disorders</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypotonia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Seizures</p></list-item><list-item><p>Developmental delay</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ocular abnormalities</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Associated with craniofacial dysmorphism</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hepatic dysfunction</p></list-item><list-item><p>Peroxisomal defects</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lacks Fanconi-type tubulopathy</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Congenital Rubella Syndrome</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Congenital cataracts</p></list-item><list-item><p>Glaucoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Growth retardation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Developmental delay</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Maternal rubella infection history</p></list-item><list-item><p>Associated cardiac anomalies and sensorineural deafness</p></list-item><list-item><p>Absent Fanconi syndrome</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Nance-Horan Syndrome</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Congenital cataracts</p></list-item><list-item><p>X-linked inheritance</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Typical facies in NHS (long, narrow face, large ears, notched teeth)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Normal kidneys and intelligence&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The severity of ocular, renal, and systemic complications largely determines the prognosis of oculocerebrorenal syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref> Visual outcomes are generally poor due to the high risk of amblyopia and the frequent development of severe, difficult-to-control glaucoma, even after cataract surgery.&#x000a0;In oculocerebrorenal syndrome, about 50% of males develop infantile glaucoma, often refractory to medical therapy, and corrected visual acuity is rarely better than 20/100.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Neuromuscular complications, particularly hypotonia, can cause significant dysphagia, sometimes necessitating tube feeding, and increase vulnerability to respiratory infections. Renal disease is a progressive condition that varies in severity, typically worsening with age. Chronic renal failure remains the leading cause of mortality, while other common contributors to reduced life expectancy include seizures, respiratory illness, and recurrent infections. Most patients survive into the second to fourth decade of life, although outcomes are highly variable and depend on early recognition, interprofessional management, and vigilant monitoring of systemic complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome involves multiple organ systems, and its complications substantially affect the overall quality of life. Ocular complications include congenital cataracts, which can cause amblyopia if untreated. Cataract surgery itself carries a risk of secondary aphakic glaucoma, which is often severe and challenging to manage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r53">[53]</xref> Neuromuscular complications, eg, hypotonia, contribute to delayed motor development, feeding difficulties, and dysphagia, increasing the risk of respiratory infections and aspiration. Neurological complications may include seizures and febrile convulsions, which can vary in severity.</p>
        <p>Renal complications arise from proximal tubular dysfunction, leading to excessive loss of minerals and electrolytes, which can cause failure to thrive, growth retardation, and hypophosphatemic rickets. Hypercalciuria increases the chances of kidney stones. Hematologic and surgical risks include impaired hemostasis and mild thrombocytopenia,&#x000a0;which may increase the likelihood of intraoperative bleeding during procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;Collectively, these complications underscore the importance of vigilant, interprofessional monitoring, early intervention, and individualized management to optimize outcomes and minimize morbidity in affected patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r46">[46]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s12" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Due to the&#x000a0;multisystem involvement of oculocerebrorenal syndrome, structured and lifelong surveillance is critical to detect complications early and optimize outcomes. To ensure good visual acuity,&#x000a0;intraocular pressure should be monitored at least every&#x000a0;6 months throughout life to detect and manage glaucoma, which may be severe and resistant to treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref> Additional eye evaluations, including visual acuity testing, corneal health assessment, and fundus examination, should be performed at intervals determined by the treating ophthalmologist, based on disease severity and surgical history.</p>
        <p>In patients undergoing cataract surgery, postoperative care and&#x000a0;rehabilitation are essential to optimize outcomes. Close and structured follow-up is necessary, primarily through serial examinations under anesthesia. These examinations under anesthesia are crucial for accurate refraction and prescription of corrective lenses, monitoring axial length growth and stabilization, and determining the optimal timing for secondary intraocular lens implantation. Since examining young or developmentally delayed children in clinic settings is often challenging, examinations under anesthesia also play a critical role in the early detection of postsurgical complications, eg, glaucoma, posterior capsular opacification, or inflammation. This tailored approach helps optimize visual outcomes and supports overall neurodevelopment in this vulnerable population.</p>
        <p>Renal monitoring is equally important, as tubular dysfunction and progression to chronic kidney disease are leading contributors to morbidity and mortality. Patients should undergo annual assessments (at least) of kidney function, including serum electrolyte levels, renal imaging, and urine studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref> Growth and developmental progress should also be tracked annually to guide interventions such as nutritional support, physiotherapy, and special education services.</p>
        <p>The increased risk of musculoskeletal complications, including scoliosis and joint laxity, warrants an annual evaluation by an orthopedic specialist.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref> This enables the timely identification of spinal deformities or joint contractures that may interfere with mobility and quality of life. Lastly, dental care is also a key component of surveillance. Semiannual dental examinations are recommended to prevent caries, manage dental structural anomalies, and promote oral health. This coordinated surveillance framework ensures early recognition of complications across multiple systems, facilitates timely interventions, and supports a proactive, interprofessional approach to patient care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Patients with oculocerebrorenal&#x000a0;syndrome require interprofessional care due to the involvement of multiple organ systems. Key consultations typically include pediatric nephrology for managing proximal renal tubular dysfunction and monitoring electrolyte imbalances, pediatric neurology for hypotonia, seizures, and developmental support, and ophthalmology for congenital cataracts, glaucoma, and other visual issues. Additional input from clinical genetics is crucial for accurate diagnosis, effective counseling, and informed family planning. Meanwhile, physical, occupational, and speech therapy support motor development and address feeding difficulties. Behavioral and psychiatric evaluation may also be necessary to address conduct disorders and adaptive functioning challenges.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r55">[55]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome is a rare X-linked recessive disorder, most commonly affecting males, with about one-third of cases resulting from de novo mutations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r56">[56]</xref> Affected males have not been reported to reproduce, but family members should be offered genetic counseling to understand inheritance patterns, recurrence risks, and implications for future pregnancies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r6">[6]</xref> Prenatal genetic testing can assist in identifying at-risk fetuses, particularly for women with previously affected children, as germline mosaicism may occur.</p>
        <p>Given the multisystemic nature of oculocerebrorenal syndrome, ongoing education for patients and caregivers is critical. Families should be informed about early complications (eg, hypotonia), which can cause feeding difficulties and delayed motor development. Awareness of potential behavioral and conduct disorders, including aggression, obsessive-compulsive tendencies, and self-injurious behaviors, is essential, particularly in children with low vision and cognitive impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r55">[55]</xref> Parents and caregivers should receive guidance on early intervention strategies, including individualized behavior modification programs, physical and occupational therapy, and appropriate pharmacologic management when indicated. Educating families on these aspects empowers them to recognize early warning signs, adhere to monitoring schedules, and actively participate in the child&#x02019;s interprofessional care, thereby helping to mitigate complications and improve long-term outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r55">[55]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome should be suspected in any male infant presenting with the triad of congenital cataracts, severe hypotonia, and evidence of proximal renal tubular dysfunction. Early ophthalmologic evaluation is crucial, as cataract extraction in infancy can significantly improve visual development and reduce the risk of amblyopia. Timely detection and management of glaucoma are also essential to preserve residual vision. Hypotonia may improve gradually with age but often results in delayed motor milestones, requiring long-term physiotherapy and supportive care.</p>
        <p>A common pitfall is misdiagnosis as isolated congenital cataracts or nonspecific developmental delay before renal features become apparent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r3">[3]</xref> Careful slit-lamp examination of carrier females can help identify at-risk families through the presence of characteristic radial lenticular opacities. Recognition of Dent disease type 2 as an allelic disorder is also essential, as it may be confused with the renal presentation of oculocerebrorenal syndrome, but lacks the extrarenal manifestations characteristic of oculocerebrorenal syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r57">[57]</xref></p>
        <p>From a preventive standpoint, genetic counseling plays a critical role. Carrier detection and prenatal or preimplantation genetic testing can inform family planning and help reduce the risk of recurrence. Interprofessional management, encompassing nephrology, ophthalmology, neurology, physiotherapy, and pediatrics, is crucial for enhancing quality of life and preventing complications,&#x000a0;eg, end-stage renal disease.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome is a rare X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> gene, leading to disruptions in phosphoinositide metabolism that affect ocular, neurological, and renal systems. The disease typically presents in infancy with congenital cataracts, hypotonia, and evolving renal dysfunction, progressing to chronic kidney disease. Because of its complex, multisystem nature, patients require lifelong,&#x000a0;interprofessional management focused on early diagnosis, prevention of complications, and optimization of function and quality of life.</p>
        <p>Management of oculocerebrorenal syndrome relies on coordinated collaboration among an interprofessional healthcare team. Physicians from ophthalmology, nephrology, neurology, and endocrinology monitor disease progression and guide evidence-based interventions. Advanced practitioners, nurses, and pharmacists support treatment adherence, manage comorbidities, and educate families. Geneticists and counselors provide essential guidance on inheritance and testing, while therapists address developmental, motor, and behavioral challenges. Effective communication, shared documentation, and ethical decision-making ensure continuity, enhance patient safety, and strengthen outcomes through cohesive, patient-centered, team-based care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140257.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140257.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
